  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how circularly polarized light can arise from chiral electronic processes and how this differs from more conventional spin-based emission. Overall, it contrasts ordinary circularly polarized luminescence, spin-LED chiroptical activity, normal chirality, and an “anomalous” mechanism tied to orbital motion.\n\nIn the top-left, circularly polarized emission is illustrated as depending on the relative orientation of electric and magnetic transition moments, shown for molecular structures with helical light waves emitted along a propagation direction. The idea is that molecular chirality can couple electric and magnetic responses to produce left- or right-handed polarized light.\n\nThe top-right compares two device concepts. A spin-LED uses magnetic contacts to inject spin-polarized carriers into an achiral emissive layer, generating circularly polarized emission. In contrast, a device with a chiral transport layer appears able to produce similar optical handedness without requiring magnetic electrodes, by inducing chirality in carrier transport before recombination in an achiral emitter.\n\nThe bottom row contrasts “normal” chiroptical activity, where a moving electron in a straight channel gives a fixed handed response, with “anomalous” chiroptical activity, where electrons travel through a helical environment. The key visual message is orbital-momentum locking: electron motion in opposite directions is linked to opposite circular polarization.",
